\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Node, saddle, and focus}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Let #{x_{*}} be a \vocabk{hyperbolic}{fnip-0034}
\vocabk{equilibrium}{fnip-0014} in the plane, and let
#{Df(x_{*})} be its \vocabk{Jacobian}{fnip-0034}. The two eigenvalues
are real, or they form a complex conjugate pair. Because the
equilibrium is hyperbolic, a complex pair has nonzero real part. The
three names record the picture those eigenvalues draw.}

\p{The equilibrium is a \newvocab{node} when both eigenvalues are real
and have the same sign. It is stable when both are negative, and
unstable when both are positive. When the eigenvalues are real and
distinct, each has an eigenvector, and that eigenvector spans an
invariant line. A solution of the linearization that starts on the
line remains on it and is a scalar multiple of #{e^{\lambda t}}.
The solutions approach or leave a node along the eigenvalue of smaller
absolute value.}

\p{The equilibrium is a \newvocab{saddle} when the eigenvalues are real
and have opposite signs. The saddle is unstable. Solutions on the
eigenline of the negative eigenvalue approach the equilibrium, and
solutions on the eigenline of the positive eigenvalue leave it.}

\p{The equilibrium is a \newvocab{focus} when the eigenvalues are a
complex conjugate pair #{\alpha\pm i\beta} with #{\beta\neq 0}.
Solutions of the linearization spiral about the equilibrium. They
decay when #{\alpha<0} and grow when #{\alpha>0}. The quantity
#{\beta} is the angular frequency of that rotation.}

\p{\citet{(1.1.6) and (1.1.7), §1.1, p. 9}{guckenheimer1983nonlinear}
give the solution #{e^{\lambda t}v} on a real eigenline and the real
pair, multiplied by #{e^{\alpha t}}, for a complex conjugate pair.
\citet{§1.2, pp. 10–11}{guckenheimer1983nonlinear}
records that a real eigenline is invariant, and that the plane spanned
by the real and imaginary parts of a complex eigenvector is invariant.
\citet{§4.2.5, pp. 104–105}{izhikevich2007dynamical}
names the node, the saddle, and the focus, including the approach
along the eigenvalue of smaller absolute value.
\citet{Theorem 2.2, p. 48}{kuznetsov2004elements}
counts eigenvalues by the sign of the real part.
\citet{Example 2.3 and Figure 2.5, pp. 48–49}{kuznetsov2004elements}
distinguish a node from a focus by whether the eigenvalues are real
or complex, and the drawings follow that distinction.}
